    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Honda Racing Releases Its First-Ever In-House Made Parts for the Civic Type R and Integra Type S\nThis Explorer Superyacht Is Equipped for Any Adventure, Packs a Submarine, Seaplane, and Helipad\n9 photos\nPhoto: Burgess/ Delta Marine\nIncorporating an extensive suite of equipment for aerial and sea exploration as well as remote-shore operations in a yacht designed to stay under the 500GT threshold is not an easy feat, but Delta Marine has accomplished this with its newest Project Zembra, an expedition superyacht that was launched in August this year and is now offered for sale. And it did so without abandoning the comforts expected of a private superyacht.\nDubbed the American shipyard’s most technically ambitious project to date, Zembra is a one-off 152-foot (46-meter) full-displacement motor yacht that combines long-range cruising with an extensive suite of exploration equipment. It’s engineered to accommodate a helicopter, a submersible, amphibious aircraft, a large tender, and other toys that facilitate exploration above and below the water.\nZembra features naval architecture and exterior design by Delta Design Group, while the interiors were created by The Roger Thomas Collection in collaboration with Woogmaster Studio and Delta Design Group. It was conceived as an adventure-forward yacht, and besides a bold, robust profile , what makes it recognizable from a distance is its full metallic Arizona bronze paint on the outside. Clean angular lines and large expanses of glazing also contribute to its distinctive appearance.\nBuilt from composite materials, the superyacht is based on a proven, full-displacement Delta Marine hull. “Zembra springs from Delta’s proven full-displacement hull platform in the 150-to 164-foot range,” reiterates Delta Vice President Michelle Jones. “This fully trans-oceanic hull design is both versatile and exceptionally capable, continuing our legacy of building true global travelers for active clients.”\nPhoto: Burgess/ Delta Marine Its exceptional inventory of exploration equipment includes a Williams DieselJet 625 tender, kayaks, Jet Skis, a Remote Operated Vehicle (ROV), paddleboards, and a modular inflatable dock system. For those interested in watching marine life, an elevated bow observation platform and an inflatable FunAir crow’s nest provide great vantage points.\nFor more daring, specialized expeditions, the superyacht has dedicated storage for a Icon A5 light-sport aircraft designed to land on water and a three-person U-Boat Worx submersible. Four cranes are included to support the handling of the extensive onboard equipment, while a scientific wet lab helps with underwater research. According to the shipbuilder, the major challenge was to make the vessel capable of supporting the additional weight of all the toys carried on the open decks.\nA custom 55-foot JR Marine catamaran shadow vessel will accompany the yacht, designed to launch and recover the four-tonne submarine. Both Zembra and the catamaran also feature helicopter landing pads, and the support catamaran has its own integrated A-frame, a hydraulic beaching bow ramp, commercial dive compressors and personal watercraft storage.\nPhoto: Burgess/ Delta Marine Besides its impressive expedition capabilities, Zembra can still function as a luxurious recreational watercraft, featuring dedicated areas for relaxation, socialization, and entertainment. An array of sumptuous living areas are strategically laid out to ensure ease of movement and daily comfort, while the finishes are carefully picked to make the interior feel warm and inviting.\nA five-stateroom layout offers accommodation for up to 12 guests, while the owner will benefit from a full-beam master suite on the main deck with panoramic floor-to ceiling windows. The en-suite guest cabins are located on the lower deck, and a fitness center can be converted into a sixth cabin if additional accommodation is needed. The yacht also includes a gym for wellness activities on board, and an elevator serves all four decks.\nAs for the convivial areas, the main salon serves as a gathering space with multiple sofas and a dining table. It opens onto an open-air lounge on the aft deck through glass doors, creating a nice indoor-outdoor entertaining space.\nThe bridge deck is dedicated to al-fresco dining, while also providing a sky lounge, and the expansive sundeck is for sunbathing and enjoying the views, with a forward jacuzzi, several seating areas, and a custom wet bar, plus another al fresco dining area.\nStyle-wise, the interior is said to be modern and inviting, with custom cerused oak, polished stainless-steel accents, Quintus furnishings, Rocky Mountain Hardware, and Tai Ping silk-and-wool carpets.\nPhoto: Burgess/ Delta Marine Zembra is powered by twin 1,200-hp Caterpillar C32 engines, which give it a cruising speed of 12 knots and a maximum speed of 15.5 knots. The vessel is equipped with Quantum QC-1800 Zero Speed stabilizers that help maintain comfort, and a pair of 99 kW Kohler generators and dual FCI watermakers support self-sufficient cruising.\nAll in all, Zembra has many aces up its sleeve, from its advanced composite construction and capability for transoceanic passages to the versatility to switch from private exploration to international charter. What’s more, it boasts Lloyd’s Register and MCA classification, which further reinforce its robust global cruising credentials.\nZembra is on the market for the first time, listed with Wes Sanford of Northrop & Johnson and Kevin Kramer of Burgess. It has an asking price of $69 million (around €61,9 million), not including the amphibious aircraft, submarine, shadow vessel and Boxfish ROV, which are all available for separate purchase.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nsuperyacht exploration yacht Zembra delta marine luxury yacht yacht design\nAbout the author: Ancuta Iosub\nAfter spending a few years as a copy editor, Ancuta decided to put down the eraser and pick up the writer's pencil. Her favorites subjects are unusual car designs, travel trailers and everything related to the great outdoors. Full profile\nUP NEXT You’ll Never Guess How Much a Ferrari LaFerrari Costs These Days",
